//! check: fail(E0701)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: std.os, comptime.sandbox
//!
//! **D33, held as a rejection: the machine's size is not a compile-time
//! constant.** `os_cpus` carries the `env` capability — the same one
//! `os_cwd` and `os_exe` carry — and the clause is explicit about why it
//! is refused in the comptime sandbox rather than merely discouraged:
//! the number is MACHINE STATE. A `comptime fn` that could read it would
//! bake the BUILDER's core count into the artifact, and a program that
//! sized its worker pool at compile time would ship a pool sized for
//! whichever machine ran `wolf add`.
//!
//! **Why the refused call is the BUILTIN and why there is no import.**
//! The same two reasons `tests/net/unix/comptime_refuses.lu` gives: a
//! `comptime fn` cannot call across a module boundary at this pin
//! (F-0051), so the refusal is witnessed one level down at the builtin
//! the facade delegates to; and `std.os` has no pure member to call at
//! run time, so an import here would have nothing to use and `E0305`
//! would fire before `E0701` could. The module is named in `conforms:`
//! instead.

comptime fn probe_cpus() -> int ! {io} {
    os_cpus()
}

fn main() -> !int {
    const N = probe_cpus() else |_| 0
    print("cpus: {N}")
    0
}
